WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.480
We think that knowledge is an endless
accumulation. You add to

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:11.639
what came before—which we do—and it never goes away; you keep adding more

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:15.859
and more and more and it builds up and
it's all getting greater and greater.

00:00:15.859 --> 00:00:25.710
Obviously not quite true. We think that
these silly people from the 17th century,

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:33.930
or the medieval folks or whatever,
are just not as smart as we are. That

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:38.309
that's why they forgot these things.
What's why they lost the recipe for

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:46.469
concrete. How could they lose it?! They must have been idiots! So we think that

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:53.280
we're wiser than people were in the past.
As Donald Rumsfeld would say, "These are

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.489
the unknown unknowns." When something is forgotten

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:07.710
we're unaware that it exists. So we have
no knowledge that, "Oh, we lost the recipe

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:13.890
for concrete." Maybe we see this dome and we go, "Oh man, how did they do that?" But we

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.780
don't know. We don't know what's missing because it's

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:26.630
missing. Abandonment, letting a knowledge
or technology go is a kind of filter.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:33.290
It might not be a good filter, but sometimes
it might be a necessary filter.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:39.689
Rediscovery, then—which is also selective—is another filter. So things are getting

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:48.030
filtered now twice. We find the future,
our own future, the future of everyone,

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:54.060
sometimes we find it in the past. Rather
than looking at the here and now and

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.439
looking forward, we should maybe turn
around and see what happened before that

00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:03.210
we missed.
So to go forwards, sometimes we first

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:12.090
have to go back. That's the end.

